Market Challenges
Manufacturing high-quality coated anodes requires specialised facilities, controlled coating processes, and skilled technical personnel. Maintaining coating uniformity and adhesion is essential because defects can affect electrode performance and service life.

The market also faces competition from alternative electrode materials in less demanding applications. Manufacturers must demonstrate the long-term operational and efficiency benefits of precious metal oxide coatings to justify their use in cost-sensitive projects.

Market Restraints
Volatility in iridium and ruthenium prices creates uncertainty in manufacturing costs and procurement budgets. Precious metals represent a significant part of coating expenses, making producers vulnerable to supply constraints and price fluctuations.

Application-specific qualification requirements can also extend purchasing timelines. Different electrolytes, operating temperatures, and current densities require suitable coating formulations, increasing testing and validation costs.

